          "p": "The strangest thing about how he got the job shapes everything after: nobody in Marcus's chain of succession was anybody's son. For most of the second century AD, Rome had been governed by what later historians call the Five Good Emperors, and they ran on a deliberate trick. Instead of leaving the empire to a biological heir (the road that produces a Caligula or a Nero, a man who inherits absolute power because of who his father was), each emperor *adopted* the ablest man he could find and made him the heir. Choose the best man, not the nearest blood. It worked, for the better part of a century, and Marcus is its product."
