          "p": "The view from above is exactly what it sounds like: a deliberate act of imagination in which Marcus lifts himself up and away and looks down on human life from a great height, until the things tormenting him shrink to their real size. He does it in *Meditations* IX.30: \"Look down from above on the countless herds of men and their countless solemnities, and the infinitely varied voyagings in storms and calms, and the differences among those who are born, who live together, and die.\" From up there, the quarrel that eats at a person, the slight he can't forgive, the honor he's desperate to win, all of it is a flicker in an ant heap. He pushes it further in the same passage, and it gets bracing: consider, he tells himself, \"how many know not even thy name, and how many will soon forget it, and how they who perhaps now are praising thee will very soon blame thee, and that neither a posthumous name is of any value, nor reputation, nor anything else.\" This is the emperor of Rome reminding himself that his fame is worth nothing, that the people cheering him now will turn, and that within a few generations almost no one will know he existed. He is not being morbid. He is cutting reputation (that classic *not*-up-to-us item) down to a size where he can stop bleeding peace onto it."
